  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Wine Tasting Form Template FAQ\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a wine tasting form template?\", \"a\": \"A wine tasting form template is a ready-made sheet or online form for scoring wines at a tasting. It gives every wine the same questions about color, aroma, taste, and finish, plus a score. Guests' notes can then be compared side by side.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a wine tasting form template?\", \"a\": \"Describe the tasting to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>: how many wines and which scoring scale. Say whether the labels stay hidden. Emergent builds the form and its database, plus a dashboard that ranks the wines. Change anything later by asking in plain English.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a wine tasting form template ask about each wine?\", \"a\": \"Ask the same things of every wine: how it looks, how it smells, how it tastes, how long the finish lasts, and a score. Put the wine's letter or name at the top. Keep each block short, because a guest tasting 6 wines answers it 6 times.\"}, {\"q\": \"What scoring scale works best on a wine tasting form template?\", \"a\": \"A 1 to 5 score works best for a casual tasting, because guests can fill it in between sips. Critics use 100 points, and Wine Spectator's scale describes 90 to 94 as \\\"Outstanding: a wine of superior character and style.\\\" Pick one scale and use it for every wine.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I run a blind tasting with a wine tasting form template?\", \"a\": \"Hide each label under foil and number the bottles. Put only those numbers on the form. Reveal the names after every score is in. Critics work this way too: at Wine Spectator, \\\"Finished wines, reviewed from bottle in blind tastings, are given a single score.\\\"\"}, {\"q\": \"Can guests fill out a wine tasting form template on their phones?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Put the link on the table or send it before the tasting, and each guest opens the form on their own phone. Every entry lands in one table, with no paper sheets to collect at the end. An Emergent form has no response caps. A 60-guest tasting fits as well as a dinner for 6.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many wines should a wine tasting form template cover?\", \"a\": \"Keep a home tasting to 6 to 8 wines. Guests then finish the form with attention left for the last glass. For a class, match the form to the wines on that night's syllabus, and add a section for each one rather than one long page.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I add up the scores from a wine tasting form template?\", \"a\": \"Total each wine's scores across all guests, or average them if some guests skipped a wine. Doing it by hand from paper takes a while after a long night. Emergent builds a dashboard that adds the scores and ranks the wines once the last guest submits.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a wine tasting form template ask guests which bottles they want to buy?\", \"a\": \"Yes, at a tasting room or a shop, where a buy option beside each wine turns a guest's notes into an order staff can fill. At a party with friends, leave it off and keep the form to tasting notes and scores.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I keep the notes from a wine tasting form template between events?\", \"a\": \"Save every entry to a database instead of paper, with the date and the wine's name on each row. Next time, you can compare how the same guests scored a grape or a producer. With Emergent, the notes live in a database you own, and paid plans export the code to your GitHub repository.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
